#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054575 :question/id #uuid "7e2e4745-f999-5b5e-bf4b-22b9596c384c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6259" :question/problem "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98c87ea4-2431-5088-8717-815efd1403ff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc16b9ab-aa9b-5770-a938-e9508ad1fce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052774} {:answer/id #uuid "f80d4806-1ba2-5afa-81f7-c44f279b170f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052775} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb2d533-72c3-568b-ad36-82015aa55185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052776} {:answer/id #uuid "191c4a6c-e51b-58a8-9229-d9c5fa9e1769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052777} {:answer/id #uuid "5acaac2a-7f38-56e0-8094-a0f037c9a0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052778}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5c4f8-c423-5a53-85f7-9121c1527dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052773}] :question/id #uuid "c18c1773-a37c-57ab-8804-e759d0096eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-40091" :question/problem "Let $p(x) = 12x^{4} + 17x^{3} - 6x^{2} - 17x - 6$. Given that $p(1) = p(-1) = 0$, find the sum of all distinct real roots of $p(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0fba6e-8356-5f44-b948-7cdd0c2e13b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054166} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5a90bb-be2b-50ed-809e-73968bdf73d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054167} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ca8fbe-6ef9-52dd-b1b4-eeb55642e3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054168} {:answer/id #uuid "34fbd4e4-4467-5934-9939-6d67da0650a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054169} {:answer/id #uuid "380d9c80-f8c2-5454-ac67-aa7512651b15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054170}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8b39bb81-4c2c-5eff-8231-762ed7dccaef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054165}] :question/id #uuid "f1886c93-7db9-5dec-b2d2-0e9d26ccf0ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-75795" :question/problem "Let $p(x) =-4x^{4} + 15x^{2} + 5x - 6$. Given that $p(2) = p(-1) = 0$, find the sum of all distinct real roots of $p(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53e4c4d3-4397-54dc-af96-ad8e22c3a161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245053} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4af89d-fba9-5c69-a7be-0fbe9b46987c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245114} {:answer/id #uuid "6b04fd81-0d0e-5c0b-916c-cf1447c159ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245263} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb2d398-ba0b-560f-8faf-2d047dffc08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245870} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc5d609-843a-5016-a708-bb20e8f2275e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecc5d609-843a-5016-a708-bb20e8f2275e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1804fba4-4426-5f7f-b256-1b34d2c01858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246588}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2583d164-46b3-52de-80d0-28acb646d7d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112638" :question/problem "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90.$ Given that $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ find the sum of all distinct real roots of $p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+3)$ and $(x-1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-35$ | $-57$ | $90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90}{x-1}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-30$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90}{x+3}={x}^{2}-x-30,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-30).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)(x-6)(x+5).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,x=6\\,,\\,x=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n1-3+6-5=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318e7ab2-c952-5845-9c5f-7e12feda6730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "318e7ab2-c952-58